,"textSignature":"{\"v\":1,\"id\":\"msg_0b220b14ab9b53290169d8f7e5f8d0819199dc8fdbb24b71c2\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"}"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_0fknQcWVz8WQlQA1RdPtze3L|fc_0b220b14ab9b53290169d8f7e6637081919b0e524b0ffe6f5e","name":"sessions_spawn","arguments":{"runtime":"subagent","mode":"run","label":"doctor-agent-diagnosis","task":"You are LoopAI Doctor Agent, a specialized diagnostic support agent. Review the following patient case and return a patient-facing diagnostic assessment as-is for me to relay. Stay within medical diagnostic support. Be clear, structured, and cautious; do not overclaim certainty. Include red flags and recommended next steps. Do not mention internal tooling.\n\nPatient ID: PT-8839-CR\n\nStored EHR / DNA context:\n- 48-year-old male\n- Hypertension / essential hypertension, previously uncontrolled\n- CKD stage 2\n- Prior BP examples: 146/92 (2025-02-14), 158/98 (2025-10-10)\n- eGFR: 82 then 71\n- UACR: 45 then 135\n- BMI 29.1\n- hs-CRP 5.2 mg/L\n- heart rate 88 bpm in cardiology record\n- Medications: lisinopril increased from 10 mg to 20 mg; amlodipine 5 mg\n- DNA / genomics: ACE rs4340 D/D, AGT rs699 T/T, APOL1 rs73885319 G/G, NOS3 rs1799983 T/T, UMOD rs4293393 A/A; high polygenic risk for hypertension and CKD progression\n\nCurrent symptom history from patient:\n- Main complaint: feeling quite exhausted lately\n- Worsening over the last few months\n- Persistent over the last month\n- Worse in the evening\n- Has not changed daily function dramatically, but normal tasks feel harder\n- Associated symptoms: some shortness of breath, dizziness, headaches, snoring\n- Dizziness sometimes occurs on standing up\n- No particular recent change in blood pressure readings, medications, supplements\n\nHRV / heart-rate data uploaded from smartwatch PDF:\n- Single session on 2026-02-15 from about 18:30 to 18:51 UTC\n- Values labeled bpm, mostly ranging about 51.3 to 59.9 bpm, generally mid-50s, with beat-to-beat variability but no obvious sustained tachycardia in the provided series\n- This appears more like resting pulse trend data than a validated clinical HRV report; interpret cautiously\n\nPlease provide:\n1. Brief synthesis\n2. Most likely explanations ranked\n3. Important cannot-miss alternatives\n4. How the HRV/heart-rate data affects interpretation\n5. What the patient should monitor or do next\n6. When the symptoms would need urgent evaluation\n\nKeep it concise but complete, suitable to send directly to the patient."},"partialJson":"{\"runtime\":\"subagent\",\"mode\":\"run\",\"label\":\"doctor-agent-diagnosis\",\"task\":\"You are LoopAI Doctor Agent, a specialized diagnostic support agent. Review the following patient case and return a patient-facing diagnostic assessment as-is for me to relay. Stay within medical diagnostic support. Be clear, structured, and cautious; do not overclaim certainty. Include red flags and recommended next steps. Do not mention internal tooling.\\n\\nPatient ID: PT-8839-CR\\n\\nStored EHR / DNA context:\\n- 48-year-old male\\n- Hypertension / essential hypertension, previously uncontrolled\\n- CKD stage 2\\n- Prior BP examples: 146/92 (2025-02-14), 158/98 (2025-10-10)\\n- eGFR: 82 then 71\\n- UACR: 45 then 135\\n- BMI 29.1\\n- hs-CRP 5.2 mg/L\\n- heart rate 88 bpm in cardiology record\\n- Medications: lisinopril increased from 10 mg to 20 mg; amlodipine 5 mg\\n- DNA / genomics: ACE rs4340 D/D, AGT rs699 T/T, APOL1 rs73885319 G/G, NOS3 rs1799983 T/T, UMOD rs4293393 A/A; high polygenic risk for hypertension and CKD progression\\n\\nCurrent symptom history from patient:\\n- Main complaint: feeling quite exhausted lately\\n- Worsening over the last few months\\n- Persistent over the last month\\n- Worse in the evening\\n- Has not changed daily function dramatically, but normal tasks feel harder\\n- Associated symptoms: some shortness of breath, dizziness, headaches, snoring\\n- Dizziness sometimes occurs on standing up\\n- No particular recent change in blood pressure readings, medications, supplements\\n\\nHRV / heart-rate data uploaded from smartwatch PDF:\\n- Single session on 2026-02-15 from about 18:30 to 18:51 UTC\\n- Values labeled bpm, mostly ranging about 51.3 to 59.9 bpm, generally mid-50s, with beat-to-beat variability but no obvious sustained tachycardia in the provided series\\n- This appears more like resting pulse trend data than a validated clinical HRV report; interpret cautiously\\n\\nPlease provide:\\n1. Brief synthesis\\n2. Most likely explanations ranked\\n3. Important cannot-miss alternatives\\n4. How the HRV/heart-rate data affects interpretation\\n5. What the patient should monitor or do next\\n6. When the symptoms would need urgent evaluation\\n\\nKeep it concise but complete, suitable to send directly to the patient.\"}"}],"api":"openai-codex-responses","provider":"openai-codex","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":5464,"output":723,"cacheRead":24064,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":30251,"cost":{"input":0.01366,"output":0.010845,"cacheRead":0.006016,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.030521}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1775826914347}}
